  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "type": "overview",
      "label": "Start",
      "title": "A softer bedside",
      "learn": [
        "denn, mal and doch are particles. Take them out and the sentence is still correct German. They only change the tone.",
        "denn in a question is friendly interest. Was heißt das denn? Wo haben Sie denn Schmerzen? It does not mean because.",
        "mal softens a short instruction. Warten Sie mal. The verb comes first, then Sie, then mal. doch encourages when someone hesitates: Kommen Sie doch.",
        "The because-denn is still the conjunction from chapter 40. Er bleibt im Bett, denn er hat Schmerzen. That denn needs the comma, and hat stays in position 2."
      ],
      "minutes": 25,
      "vocab": [
        { "de": "Schmerz", "article": "der", "plural": "die Schmerzen", "en": "pain", "emoji": "😣", "glossaryKey": "schmerzen" },
        { "de": "Bett", "article": "das", "plural": "die Betten", "en": "bed", "emoji": "🛏️", "image": { "type": "svg", "id": "bed" }, "glossaryKey": "bett" },
        { "de": "Tablette", "article": "die", "plural": "die Tabletten", "en": "tablet", "emoji": "💊", "glossaryKey": "tablette" }
      ],
      "text": {
        "title": "Was heißt das denn?",
        "de": "Was heißt das denn? Wo haben Sie denn Schmerzen? Warten Sie mal. Kommen Sie doch. Er bleibt im Bett, denn er hat Schmerzen.",
        "en": "What does that mean, then? Where does it hurt? Just wait a moment. Do come. He stays in bed because he has pain."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar",
      "type": "grammar",
      "label": "Grammar",
      "title": "denn, mal, doch",
      "practice": "choose",
      "heroTitle": "Was heißt das denn?",
      "heroImage": "bed",
      "story": [
        "A particle sits in the middle of a sentence you already know. <b>Was heißt das?</b> is a complete question. <b>Was heißt das denn?</b> is the same question, only friendlier. das is that, not dass.",
        "Question-<b>denn</b> is not the because-<b>denn</b>. The because-denn links two clauses and keeps the verb in position 2: <b>Er bleibt im Bett, denn er hat Schmerzen.</b> You cannot delete that denn. You can delete the question-denn.",
        "<b>mal</b> softens a routine instruction. It means just, for a moment. <b>Warten Sie mal. Trinken Sie mal Wasser.</b> The formal imperative is verb, then Sie, then mal. Not Warten mal Sie.",
        "<b>doch</b> encourages when the patient hesitates. <b>Kommen Sie doch. Setzen Sie sich doch.</b> Again Sie comes before the particle. In a calm statement it reassures: Das ist doch nur eine Tablette.",
        "After a negative question, <b>Doch</b> means yes, on the contrary. Haben Sie keine Schmerzen? <b>Doch, ich habe Schmerzen.</b> Ja does not answer that negative question. Nein would mean no pain.",
        "Was heißt denn das? is also a natural question. Was denn heißt das? is not: the verb would leave position 2."
      ],
      "questionRule": "Question-denn only softens. mal is a short instruction. doch encourages, or answers a negative question. Because-denn keeps hat in position 2.",
      "tables": [
        {
          "title": "Drei Partikeln",
          "caption": "The particle is optional. The rest of the sentence stays.",
          "columns": ["Ton", "Satz"],
          "rows": [
            { "label": "denn", "cells": ["freundliche Frage", "Was heißt das denn?"], "highlight": true },
            { "label": "mal", "cells": ["kurzer Imperativ", "Warten Sie mal."], "highlight": true },
            { "label": "doch", "cells": ["Ermutigung", "Kommen Sie doch."] }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Zwei denn, und Doch",
          "caption": "Question-denn has no comma. Because-denn has a comma, and hat stays in position 2. Doch answers a negative question.",
          "columns": ["Job", "Satz"],
          "rows": [
            { "label": "Frage", "cells": ["Partikel", "Was heißt das denn?"], "highlight": true },
            { "label": "Grund", "cells": ["Konjunktion", "Er bleibt im Bett, denn er hat Schmerzen."], "highlight": true },
            { "label": "Antwort", "cells": ["verneinte Frage", "Doch, ich habe Schmerzen."] }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Die richtige Stelle",
          "caption": "The verb stays in position 2. In the formal imperative, Sie stands before mal or doch.",
          "columns": ["Falsch", "Richtig"],
          "rows": [
            { "label": "denn", "cells": ["Was denn heißt das?", "Was heißt das denn?"] },
            { "label": "mal", "cells": ["Warten mal Sie.", "Warten Sie mal."] },
            { "label": "doch", "cells": ["Kommen doch Sie.", "Kommen Sie doch."] }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "warning": {
        "title": "Four particle traps",
        "items": [
          { "de": "Was heißt das denn?", "en": "Friendly question. Verb in position 2.", "tone": "ok" },
          { "de": "Was denn heißt das?", "en": "denn is not in front of the verb.", "tone": "stop" },
          { "de": "Warten Sie mal.", "en": "Sie, then mal.", "tone": "ok" },
          { "de": "Warten mal Sie.", "en": "mal does not split the imperative from Sie.", "tone": "stop" },
          { "de": "Kommen Sie doch.", "en": "Encouragement.", "tone": "ok" },
          { "de": "Kommen doch Sie.", "en": "doch follows Sie.", "tone": "stop" },
          { "de": "Doch, ich habe Schmerzen.", "en": "Yes, after a negative question.", "tone": "ok" },
          { "de": "Ja, ich habe Schmerzen.", "en": "Ja does not contradict keine.", "tone": "stop" }
        ],
        "tip": "Question: denn. A moment: mal. Encouragement, or yes after keine: doch. Because: denn er hat."
      },
